From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Mon, 8 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] software_isp: af: Add AfMode, use dioptres, hold a manual position

The soft ISP autofocus added in 0015 self-triggers: Af::step() treats any
sharpness change of more than 30% as "focus lost" and restarts a sweep.
That is what makes it work in applications with no AF support at all, but
it also means a position set through LensPosition survives only until the
scene changes, so manual focus is not usable today.

Register controls::AfMode with AfModeManual and AfModeContinuous,
defaulting to continuous so existing behaviour is unchanged, and return
early from Af::step() in manual mode so nothing sweeps the lens away from
where the application put it.

Along the way:

  - Pass the focus lens ControlInfoMap to the IPA at init() time, not only
    at configure() time, so Af::init() can decline to register any focus
    control when the sensor has no actuator. Without this a fixed-focus
    sensor still advertises LensPosition and applications draw a focus
    slider that cannot move anything.

  - Fix the AfTrigger polarity. AfTriggerStart is 0 and AfTriggerCancel is
    1, but the code started a sweep on 1 and stopped one on 0. Advertise
    the control as an enum of the two values rather than a 0..1 range so
    that applications, and the PipeWire SPA bridge, present it as a
    choice.

  - Report AfMode in the metadata, and report AfStateIdle rather than
    AfStateFocused while in manual mode, as the specification requires.

  - Drop the const from the CameraSensor the SoftwareIsp constructor
    takes, as CameraSensor::focusLens() is not const. The only caller
    already holds a non-const sensor.

  - Express LensPosition in dioptres, as the control specifies, instead of
    on an invented 0..100 scale. An application that follows the spec and
    asks for 0.5 to focus at 2m was previously served a position five
    codes off the bottom of the actuator's travel, indistinguishable from
    infinity; it now gets what it asked for. Positions are dioptres
    throughout the algorithm, including the sweep, and are converted to
    actuator codes in one place, through a piecewise-linear map. The
    sweep's step sizes become fractions of the focus range rather than
    the hardcoded 2 and 0.2, which keeps the coarse-to-fine search
    identical in shape whatever the range is.

    The map is read from the sensor's tuning file using libipa's Pwl and
    the same flat x,y list schema as the Raspberry Pi IPA's "rpi.af" map,
    so a calibration measured for one can be transcribed to the other:

        - Af:
            map: [ 0.0, 100, 10.0, 1000 ]
            range: { min: 0.0, max: 10.0, default: 1.0 }

    No tuning file carries a map yet, so the code falls back to assuming
    the actuator spans infinity to 10cm linearly and logs a warning that
    reported distances are not trustworthy. That fallback reproduces the
    previous behaviour exactly, just relabelled; making it correct needs
    a bench calibration of the module, which is a physical measurement
    rather than a code change.

  - Offset the lens position by focus_min when mapping it onto the
    actuator range. This is a no-op for the ak7375 on starqltechn, whose
    range starts at 0, but it is wrong for any actuator that does not.
